act†info.arxiv.org】 · 【60†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【61†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【62†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【63†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【64†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL162: \nL163: Major funding support from\nL164: \nL165: 【65†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【66†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【67†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3: (500) Redirected to https://idp.nature.com/authorize?response_type=cookie&client_id=grover&redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-17126-3 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/bitstreams/d836f6f4-ed5f-4ec6-b31e-f358bb36b323/download)\nciteturn10view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 19; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view3\",\"id\":1}); Total lines: 586\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: The role of CO2 capture and utilisation in mitigating climate change\nL2@P0: Authors: Niall Mac Dowella,b*, Paul S. Fennellc, Nilay Shahb,c and Geoffrey C. Maitlandb,c\nL3@P0: Affiliation:\nL4@P0: a: Centre for Environmental Policy, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7\nL5@P0: 1NA\nL6@P0: b: Centre for Process Systems Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus,\nL7@P0: London SW7 2AZ\nL8@P0: c: Department of Chemical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London\nL9@P0: SW7 2AZ\nL10@P0: * Corresponding author: niall@imperial.ac.uk\nL11@P0: Author contributions:\nL12@P0: All authors contributed to the planning of the paper. NMD led the work, benefiting from discussions\nL13@P0: with all authors. All authors contributed to writing the Commentary, providing comments to the\nL14@P0-1: framework and input in terms of numbers and references backing the analysis.2\nL15@P1: Abstract\nL16@P1: The challenges associated with climate change mitigation are vast. In this commentary, we put\nL17@P1: in perspective the potential contribution of CO2 utilisation, distinct to CO2 sequestration, to\nL18@P1: climate change mitigation. With a view to offsetting the cost associated with CO2 capture and\nL19@P1: sequestration (CCS), there has been a growing interest in finding commercially viable end use\nL20@P1: opportunities for the captured CO2. Owing to the scale and rate at which CO2 is produced and\nL21@P1: at which it can be utilised in a form corresponding to its long-term sequestration, it is highly\nL22@P1: improbable the chemical conversion of CO2 will account for more than 1% of the mitigation\nL23@P1: challenge and even a scaled up CO2-EOR-CCS industry will likely only account for 4-8%.\nL24@P1: Therefore, whilst CO2-EOR may be an important economic incentive for some early CCS\nL25@P1: projects, CCU may prove to be a financially and politically costly distraction from the real task\nL26@P1: of mitigating dangerous climate change.\nL27@P1: Introduction\nL28@P1: The continued growth in anthropogenic CO2 emissions would appear to be characterised by\nL29@P1: one word – inexorable. Despite a growing number of climate change mitigation policies,\nL30@P1: anthropogenic CO2 emissions in the period 2000 to 2014 grew at an average rate of 2.6%/yr,\nL31@P1: in contrast with an average rate of 1.72%/yr in the period 1970 – 2000 [1, 2]. Indeed, in the\nL32@P1: period 2010 to 2014, emissions increased from approximately 31.9 to 35.5 GtCO2/yr; an average\nL33@P1: rate of 2.75%/yr [2]. With the exception of a one-year reduction from 2008 to 2009, every year\nL34@P1: of this century has seen a year on year increase in anthropogenic CO2 emissions.\nL35@P1: It has become commonplace to discuss future emission trajectories in terms of scenarios from,\nL36@P1: for example, the International Energy Agency (IEA) or the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate\nL37@P1: Change (IPCC). Both the IEA and IPCC project that a world commensurate no more than 2oC\nL38@P1: of warming above pre-industrial levels is one in which total anthropogenic CO2 emissions are\nL39@P1: reduced to something less than 20 GtCO2/yr by 2050 with further reductions to near-zero or\nL40@P1: even net-negative emissions by the end of the century. This is typically referred to as the “two\nL41@P1: degree scenario” or “2DS”. On the other end of the spectrum, allowing anthropogenic\nL42@P1: emissions to increase to 60 GtCO2/yr by 2050 is commensurate with warming of approximately\nL43@P1: 6\nL44@P1: oC above pre-industrial levels – this is the “six degree scenario” or “6DS”[1, 3].\nL45@P1: The conclusion one can draw from the foregoing data is that if anthropogenic emissions of CO2\nL46@P1-2: continue on any of the recent growth trends, we are poised to very significantly overshoot the 3\nL47@P2: six degree scenario (6DS). To even meet the 6DS, we would need to reduce the annual rate of\nL48@P2: growth of emissions to 1.4% and to meet the 2DS, the rate of growth needs to be -1.5% - if\nL49@P2: global emissions peak in the 2020’s. If emissions peak later, the required rate of reduction\nL50@P2: similarly increases. For the remainder of this analysis, we hypothesise a world, inspired by\nL51@P2: recent success in Paris, that reduces emissions to a level commensurate with a 6DS world by\nL52@P2: 2020 and aims thereafter to transition to a world commensurate with the 2DS, focusing on the\nL53@P2: period to 2050. This allows us to introduce the quantity “mitigation challenge”, 𝑀𝐶, i.e., the\nL54@P2: amount of avoided CO2 emissions (against a reference case) by a given date, 𝑡𝑓, in order to\nL55@P2: reduce emissions to a level commensurate with meeting the 2DS, 𝐸\nL56@P2: 2𝐷𝑆\nL57@P2: , which is a function of\nL58@P2: the year in which emissions peak, 𝑡𝑝, the emission rate in that year, 𝐸\nL59@P2: 𝑡𝑝, and lastly the rate at\nL60@P2: which CO2 would be emitted in 𝑡𝑓 according to a low mitigation scenario (LMS) reference\nL61@P2: scenario, 𝐸\nL62@P2: 𝐿𝑀𝑆\nL63@P2: .\nL64@P2: Figure 1: Illustration of the calculation of the mitigation challenge. Here, historical data is sourced from BPs data [2], the\nL65@P2: BAU scenario chosen here is the IEA’s 6DS and the objective is to meet the IEA’s 2DS for 2050 [3]. In this example, the MC\nL66@P2-3: equates to approximately 800 GtCO2 in the period to 2050.4\nL67@P3: Therefore, 𝑀𝐶 can be expressed as 𝑀𝐶 =\nL68@P3: (𝑡𝑓−𝑡𝑝)(𝐸\nL69@P3: 𝐿𝑀𝑆(𝑡𝑝)−𝐸2𝐷𝑆)\nL70@P3: 2\nL71@P3: . In addition to being a\nL72@P3: function of 𝑡𝑓, 𝐸\nL73@P3: 𝐿𝑀𝑆 is also a function of 𝑡𝑝, and the average rate of growth of anthropogenic\nL74@P3: CO2 associated with the LMS scenario in the period (𝑡𝑓 − 𝑡𝑝). Therefore, 𝐸\nL75@P3: 𝐿𝑀𝑆(𝑡𝑝) =\nL76@P3: 𝐸\nL77@P3: 𝑡𝑝(1 + 𝑟)\nL78@P3: (𝑡𝑓−𝑡𝑝)\nL79@P3: . Thus, in order to meet the IEA’s 2DS with the 6DS as a baseline, it is\nL80@P3: necessary to avoid the cumulative emission of approximately 800 GtCO2 in the period to 2050.\nL81@P3: Globally, despite an increasing emphasis on renewable energy, annual investment in fossil\nL82@P3: energy has more than doubled in real terms in the period 2000 to 2013, totalling more than\nL83@P3: $950 billion at the end of this period [4]. It is therefore not unreasonable to suggest that fossil\nL84@P3: fuels will continue to be important to, if not dominate, the world’s energy landscape for some\nL85@P3: time to come, with some estimates indicating that fossil fuels will still account for over 65% of\nL86@P3: the total energy mix in 2100 [5], despite increasing penetration of renewable electricity\nL87@P3: generation [6]. For this energy mix to be coherent with the long-term ambition of substantially\nL88@P3: mitigating anthropogenic CO2 emissions, the widespread deployment of CO2 capture and\nL89@P3: storage (CCS) technology [7-9] will most likely be a vital part of the least-cost energy system\nL90@P3: of the future, working in conjunction with renewable energy to deliver energy which is low\nL91@P3: carbon, available and affordable.\nL92@P3: From one perspective, CCS is a readily deployable technology solution, relying on well\u0002understood components [7-9]. Two leading options for decarbonising both the power and\nL93@P3: industrial sectors are the oxy-combustion of fuel or post-combustion scrubbing of the exhaust\nL94@P3: gas arising from a conventional combustion process. Both of these technologies are highly\nL95@P3: mature. Alkanolamine gas scrubbing was first patented in the 1930s and has since been widely\nL96@P3: used for natural gas sweetening [10]. Oxy-combustion, which relies on the cryogenic\nL97@P3: separation of air, was developed by Linde in 1902 and was operating at the 30,000 metric\nL98@P3: tonnes of oxygen per day (MTPD) scale at the Shell Pearl GTL project in Qatar in 2006. This\nL99@P3: is sufficient oxygen to supply a 2 GW oxy-combustion power plant. Similarly, CO2 transport\nL100@P3: and injection has been practiced at scale for enhanced oil recovery (EOR) since the 1950’s. As\nL101@P3: of 2014, there are over 3000 miles of high-pressure pipeline which transport over 60,000,000\nL102@P3: tonnes of CO2 per year for Enhanced Oil Recovery (EOR) in 113 projects in the United States\nL103@P3: alone, with approximately 120 projects worldwide [11, 12]. Similarly, the distribution and\nL104@P3: capacity of CO2 storage locations are also reasonably well characterised with first order\nL105@P3: estimates of theoretical global CO2 storage capacity of approximately 11,000 GtCO2[13]. Of\nL106@P3-4: this, approximately 1,000 GtCO2 capacity is provided by oil and gas reservoirs with 5\nL107@P4: approximately 9,000 – 10,000 GtCO2 capacity provided by deep saline aquifers[14-16]. Further,\nL108@P4: there is also significant potential capacity in unminaable coal seams, with the additional\nL109@P4: economic benefit that this is may be accompanied by the recovery of coal-bed methane.\nL110@P4: In order to stabilize atmospheric CO2 concentrations at a level of 450 ppm, i.e., a concentration\nL111@P4: consistent with a world with a high likelihood of not exceeding 2\nL112@P4: oC of warming, it is expected\nL113@P4: that it will be necessary to store 120 – 160 GtCO2 via CCS in the period to 2050 [17], with\nL114@P4: similar trends expected to the end of the century. Therefore we have more than enough CO2\nL115@P4: storage capacity to meet this target and, even without identification of further storage sinks,\nL116@P4: sufficient to meet even ambitious CO2 sequestration needs for well beyond the next century;\nL117@P4: giving ample time for the likely lengthy transition from fossil fuels. Finally, the world’s first\nL118@P4: commercial CCS-equipped power station has started operation at the Boundary Dam facility\nL119@P4: in Saskatchewan, Canada with a second project also in operation in Alberta where Shell are\nL120@P4: capturing the CO2 arising from H2 production [18]. CCS is inarguably a well understood,\nL121@P4: mature technology that is deployable at commercial scale today.\nL122@P4: However, despite CCS relying on well-known and understood technology components, the\nL123@P4: transition to its widespread deployment continues to be an uphill battle. The financing of this\nL124@P4: transition is a particular challenge, one which requires the combination of strong policy and\nL125@P4: price signals to ensure that low-carbon and energy efficiency investments offer a sufficiently\nL126@P4: attractive risk-adjusted return.\nL127@P4: It is in this context that CO2 utilisation (CCU) is often mentioned. As a relatively benign\nL128@P4: material, it is possible to convert CO2 into a wide variety of end products, in addition to its\nL129@P4: potential for enhanced hydrocarbon recovery. In this context, therefore, why should we not\nL130@P4: actively and favourably consider the reuse of captured CO2?\nL131@P4: Certainly it represents a beguiling opportunity – convert a waste product into high value end\nL132@P4: products, kick-starting a high-skills regional manufacturing industry. Moreover, global demand\nL133@P4: for the potential products, such as methanol, appears healthy [19].\nL134@P4: Therefore, it is easy to see why the prospect of CO2 utilisation is an attractive one for a wide\nL135@P4: variety of academic, industrial and political stakeholders. However, serious questions arise\nL136@P4: when the narrative around CO2 utilisation becomes one of “utilisation in parallel to storage” or\nL137@P4-5: “utilisation instead of storage”. As will be discussed subsequently in this paper, from the 6\nL138@P5: perspective of mitigating anthropogenic climate change, CO2 utilisation is highly unlikely to\nL139@P5: ever be a realistic alternative to long term, secure, geologic sequestration.\nL140@P5: The remainder of this paper is laid out as follows; we first discuss the scale at which various\nL141@P5: CCU options could be deployed, we then go on to discuss the rate at which they could be\nL142@P5: deployed before finally discussing how much of the CO2 used in the various options correspond\nL143@P5: to permanent storage. In all cases, this is contextualised with reference to the aforementioned\nL144@P5: mitigation challenge.\nL145@P5: It’s a matter of scale\nL146@P5: To put this in some perspective, current total global anthropogenic emissions are about 35.5\nL147@P5: GtCO2/yr. Typical CO2 injection and storage conditions are approximately 10 MPa and 40oC,\nL148@P5: corresponding to a CO2 density of approximately 600 kg/m3. This corresponds to\nL149@P5: approximately 1.64 ×108 m3/day, or more than 1,033 million barrels (Mbbl) of CO2 per day.\nL150@P5: This is in contrast to current global oil production rates of approximately 87– 91Mbbl per day\nL151@P5: [20, 21]. This means that global CO2 production today is approximately a factor of 10 greater\nL152@P5: than global oil production today, and, at current rates of growth, may be as much as a factor of\nL153@P5: 20 greater in 2050 [22].\nL154@P5: Given that CCS is expected to account for the mitigation of approximately 14 - 20% of total\nL155@P5: anthropogenic CO2 emissions, in 2050 the CCS industry will need to be larger by a factor of\nL156@P5: 2-4 in volume terms than the current global oil industry. In other words, we have 35 years to\nL157@P5: deploy an industry that is substantially larger than one which has been developed over\nL158@P5: approximately the last century, resulting in the sequestration of 8 - 10 Gt CO2 per annum by\nL159@P5: 2050 [22] with a cumulative CO2 storage target of approximately 120 - 160 Gt CO2 in the\nL160@P5: period to 2050 [17] and between 1,200 - 3,300 Gt CO2 over the course of the 21st century [13].\nL161@P5: This is an exceptionally challenging task, similar in scale to wartime mobilisation, but one from\nL162@P5: which we should not be daunted. Neither should we be distracted by focusing too much on the\nL163@P5: long term solution without giving sufficient attention to the short-to-medium term necessity of\nL164@P5: fossil fuel decarbonisation in a manner the allows them to operate in sympathy with intermittent\nL165@P5: generation from renewable sources [23].\nL166@P5: It is important to note that when CO2 utilisation has traditionally been discussed, this has been\nL167@P5: in the context of CO2-Enhanced Oil Recovery (CO2-EOR) in the United States. In this paper\nL168@P5-6: we include CO2-EOR within a definition that considers any use of CO2, physical or chemical,7\nL169@P6: that prevents immediate release of CO2 to the atmosphere as part of CCU. EOR is already a\nL170@P6: very mature technology with a history reaching back several decades, having well defined\nL171@P6: techno-economic parameters, and is often considered to be an important part of the CCU\nL172@P6: landscape. In the early years of its development, CO2-EOR faced the challenge of relatively\nL173@P6: low oil prices and relatively high CO2 prices. Reservoir management was therefore optimised\nL174@P6: to maximise profit, not CO2 sequestration. At the time of writing, CO2-EOR provides\nL175@P6: approximately 5% percent of the total U.S. crude oil production [24], and whilst it has the\nL176@P6: potential to be appreciably expanded [25], it is important to note the relationship between CO2\nL177@P6: price and oil price. At oil prices of approximately $100/bbl, CO2 needs to be available at less\nL178@P6: than $45/tonne [12] for CCS-EOR to be economically viable. This is the case in the United\nL179@P6: States, where the business model is very mature and the CO2-EOR capacity exists onshore, and\nL180@P6: may not hold for the rest of the world. Thus, current oil prices in the range of $40 - 60/bbl and\nL181@P6: CO2 costs of $60 - 80/tonne [26, 27], makes CO2-EOR less viable as a means of balancing the\nL182@P6: costs of large scale CCS operations, and separate economic or policy incentives are likely to\nL183@P6: be required.\nL184@P6: Nevertheless, there is little question that CO2-EOR offers a large, near-term option to store\nL185@P6: large quantities of CO2 at lower net cost, with more than 90% of the world’s oil reservoirs\nL186@P6: seemingly suitable for CO2-EOR [12], if treated early enough before the reservoir pressure\nL187@P6: drops below the minimum miscibility pressure. Thus, there exists the theoretical potential to\nL188@P6: produce 470 billion bbl of additional oil, corresponding to a cumulative theoretical CO2\nL189@P6: injection capacity in the range of 70 -140 Gt [12, 28].\nL190@P6: However, this may be a highly optimistic estimate of the total deployable CO2-EOR capacity.\nL191@P6: As illustrated in Figure 2, the majority of this capacity exists in the Middle East and North\nL192@P6: Africa and in the USA at 50% and 13% respectively, whereas the estimated CO2-EOR in South\nL193@P6-7: Asia is essentially zero and the Asia Pacific region accounting for only about 3%. 8\nL194@P7: Figure 2: Global CO2-EOR capacity compared with regional CO2 sequestration targets. Data from [13, 17, 22]. The error\nL195@P7: bars included on this data indicate an average calculated variance of 30%. The reported variance is in the range 25 – 35%.\nL196@P7: In other words, as illustrated in Figure 2, there appears to be an unfortunate disconnect between\nL197@P7: regions of substantial CO2-EOR potential and those regions with the largest anticipated\nL198@P7: population growth, dependence on fossil fuels and hence requirement to sequester CO2 over\nL199@P7: the course of the next century. In fact, the only region where it appears certain that there is\nL200@P7: sufficient CO2-EOR capacity to meet the CO2 storage requirements to 2050 is the Middle East\nL201@P7: and Africa – although the requirements are close in North America and the Former Soviet\nL202@P7: Union. Given the size and rate of growth of the CO2-EOR industry in the USA, it is likely that\nL203@P7: the USA will be a leader in the deployment of CO2-EOR. If we accept the availability of a\nL204@P7: CCS-derived stream of CO2 as a prerequisite for CO2-EOR, it would make sense to estimate\nL205@P7: the scale of likely CO2-EOR activities as matching regional CCS targets. Thus, a more realistic\nL206@P7: estimate is likely to be on the order of 40 Gt cumulative CO2 injected for CO2-EOR. Thereafter,\n"
